<DocsLayout title="Quickstart" description="Install and run Light WAF in two minutes: install the waf binary (or pull the container image), write a minimal config, and watch it block an attack.">
  <h1>Quickstart</h1>
  <p class="lede">Get the WAF running in front of a backend in about two minutes — with cargo, or with the container image.</p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>Install the <code>waf</code> binary straight from crates.io (needs Rust and cargo):</p>
  <pre class="code"><span class="p">$</span> cargo install waf-proxy</pre>
  <p>Or build from source:</p>
  <pre class="code"><span class="p">$</span> git clone {REPO}
<span class="p">$</span> cd Light-WAF &amp;&amp; cargo build --release -p waf-proxy</pre>

  <h2>Configure</h2>
  <p>Create a <code>config.toml</code> with a listener and your backend. This is the smallest useful config:</p>
  <pre class="code"><span class="c"># config.toml</span>
[proxy]
listen  = "0.0.0.0:8080"
backend = "http://127.0.0.1:3000"

[waf]
mode = "blocking"        <span class="c"># "blocking" enforces | "detection-only" observes and logs</span>
block_threshold = 5      <span class="c"># deny when the anomaly score reaches this</span></pre>
  <p>
    Tuning a new deployment? Start with <code>mode = "detection-only"</code>: every verdict is computed
    and logged, but nothing is blocked. The repository's <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/examples`}>examples</a>
    have ready profiles from detection-only to strict. See the <a href="/docs/configuration">configuration
    reference</a> for every section.
  </p>

  <h2>Run</h2>
  <pre class="code"><span class="p">$</span> waf --config config.toml
<span class="c"># or, from a source checkout:</span>
<span class="p">$</span> cargo run --release -p waf-proxy -- --config config.toml</pre>
  <p>The WAF listens on <code>:8080</code> and forwards allowed requests to your backend.</p>

  <h3>With the container image</h3>
  <p>
    The <code>waf-enterprise</code> image runs the same config — every at-scale component is off until
    you enable it. It reads <code>/etc/waf/config.toml</code> by default. Inside the container
    <code>127.0.0.1</code> is the container itself, so point <code>backend</code> at an address the
    container can reach (for a backend on the Docker host: <code>http://host.docker.internal:3000</code>).
  </p>
  <pre class="code"><span class="p">$</span> docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 \
    -v "$PWD/config.toml:/etc/waf/config.toml:ro" \
    {IMAGE_REGISTRY}/waf-enterprise:{VERSION}</pre>

  <h2>Verify</h2>
  <p>A benign request is forwarded; an obvious attack is blocked with a <code>403</code>:</p>
  <pre class="code"><span class="p">$</span> curl -o /dev/null -w "%&#123;http_code&#125;\n" localhost:8080/
<span class="ok">200</span>
<span class="p">$</span> curl -o /dev/null -w "%&#123;http_code&#125;\n" "localhost:8080/?q=1'+OR+'1'='1"
<span class="no">403</span></pre>

